The change is in `retire()` in `reef/execution/executor/process_guard.py`, plus the Slime resources code that logs its result. I didn't open either file, so the choice rests on the grep results alone.

The grep for `def retire(` found `retire(token, timeout_s)` only in `process_guard.py`. The other `retire` methods are on unrelated classes (judging, inference residency, recovery, vllm, sglang). The grep for `process_guard` found `reef/train/slime_backend/resources.py` and `reef/train/slime_backend/rollout_group.py` as callers. I put `resources.py` ahead of `rollout_group.py` because the change says "the Slime resources log them". I rank `rollout_group.py` last because I didn't check whether it calls `retire()`.

{"files": ["reef/execution/executor/process_guard.py", "reef/train/slime_backend/resources.py", "reef/train/slime_backend/rollout_group.py"]}

The fix belongs in `TaskRolloutRunner.rollout_score` in `reef/harness/tasks/rollouts.py`. Only the evaluation path calls it, so training rollouts stay as they are. The change makes it return `RolloutScore.failed()` when the rollout had no answered model call.

I did not make the edit, and I did not read how the evaluation worker configures the runner, so I'm assuming evaluation rollouts use the unrecorded (rollout-header) path. `reef/evaluation/rollout_worker.py` is the only other file that might need a change.

{"files": ["reef/harness/tasks/rollouts.py", "reef/evaluation/rollout_worker.py"]}

The change is in `SQLDataStore.create`, which the locate results put at `reef/storage/sql_data.py` lines 401-460. I haven't read that code. The ranking comes from the trailmark locate output alone.

The other files were less likely. `reef/storage/postgres.py` and `reef/storage/sqlite.py` supply the database-specific insert and lock hooks. They would change only if the set statements need dialect-specific syntax.

{"files": ["reef/storage/sql_data.py", "reef/storage/postgres.py", "reef/storage/sqlite.py"]}

The change belongs in `startup_status` in `reef/service/inference_service.py` (around lines 256–290). I read that function but haven't edited anything.

It reads the current serving request for the scenario and turns that request's state into the model's status. A model that sits beside the active one gets the whole request's `starting` or `failed` state. The fix has to check first whether a published route already serves the artifact, and return `ready` if so. That check can use the existing `resolve` method or the cache. I did not trace how `resolve` looks up routes, so I don't know whether `served_models.py` needs a change too. It is the less likely second file.

{"files": ["reef/service/inference_service.py", "reef/storage/served_models.py"]}

The change is in `InferenceReplica.startup_status`, in `reef/service/inference_service.py`. It computes the state from the serving request alone, whatever model was asked for. The fix is to read `ready` when a published route of that model already serves the artifact. I read the locate output only and did not open the file, so I haven't checked how it finds the published routes.

The route lookup may need a change in `reef/storage/served_models.py`, which holds the request and route table that `startup_status` reads. I'm listing it as less likely.

{"files": ["reef/service/inference_service.py", "reef/storage/served_models.py"]}

The fix is in `write_job_data` in `reef/storage/job_data.py` (lines 68–93). It builds a list of every line, joins them into one string, then encodes that string. That holds roughly three copies of the file in memory. Writing each line to an open file handle as it is produced removes the copies.

I didn't edit any files or run any tests.

{"files": ["reef/storage/job_data.py"]}

The change description is vague, so this is a low-confidence guess. I did not open any of the files. The ranking comes from the Trailmark search, and the ordering is a guess from the names of the examples it matched.

The `setup` method in the SAO example agent creates the scenario before use, and the GEPA AIME example builds its own feedback and provider endpoint. Those are the closest matches for "example setup and feedback reporting". The SDPO and OpenClawRL agents also have a `setup` method, so they are plausible alternatives.

